.TH TIMBERFS-RECORDS 5 "2026-07-12" "timberfs" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
timberfs-records \- typed, NUL-framed log-entry record stream
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B timberfs query \-\-records
.I ...
| consumer
.SH DESCRIPTION
The record stream is the pipe interface between timberfs selection
(\fBtimberfs query\fR) and entry-aware consumers. It carries whole log
ENTRIES (a stamped line plus its continuation lines) as verbatim bytes,
interleaved with typed metadata records. It is a byte stream, not a
text format: read it with a parser, not with line-oriented tools (for
those, use
.B \-0
\(em bare NUL-terminated entries \(em or plain text).
.SH GRAMMAR
.nf
.RS
stream  := record*
record  := body NUL
body    := RS kind (US key=value)*    metadata record
         | payload                    entry payload, verbatim bytes
.RE
.fi
.PP
.B RS
is byte 0x1E (ASCII record separator),
.B US
is 0x1F (unit separator),
.B NUL
is 0x00. A record whose first byte is RS is metadata; every other
record is the verbatim payload of the entry announced by the
immediately preceding
.B entry
metadata record. Keys never contain \(lq=\(rq; the first \(lq=\(rq
splits key from value. Values never contain NUL or US (timberfs
refuses to emit such a stream). Payload bytes are arbitrary \(em
including NUL: the
.B len
field of the
.B entry
record is authoritative, and the payload's closing NUL is a
consistency and resynchronization marker, not the framing.
.SH RECORD KINDS
.TP
.B stream-start
First record of every stream. Fields:
.B v
(format version, this document describes
.BR v=1 ),
.B server_version
(WHAT produced this answer, product and version: \(lqtimberfs, 0.24.0\(rq.
An answer outlives the connection that fetched it \(em relayed, piped,
kept \(em and a consumer finding one that behaves oddly should not have
to infer the version from the behaviour. The product is named because the
thing answering need not be a timberfs),
.B from
/
.B to
(the selection window in Unix milliseconds; omitted edges are
unbounded),
.B has
(repeated; the chunk-level token requirements of the selection),
.B any
(repeated; chunk-level OR alternatives of the selection),
.B sources
(number of inputs),
.B order
(how entries from different stores are arranged \(em see
.B ORDERING
below),
.B stage
(repeated; each pipeline stage that transformed the stream appends its
own command line \(em provenance accumulates across the pipe). The
selection echo and the stage list let a downstream tool record the
full lineage of what it consumed.
.TP
.B source
One per input, in argument order, before any entries. Fields:
.B path
(the source as given),
.B id
(the store's IDENTITY \(em what a request selects on and what a position
is recorded against; absent for a store with no manifest, and for a
stream that did not come from a store),
.B kept
/
.B total
(chunks selected vs present \(em what the index did for this stream;
a consumer can warn \(lqthis will be slow\(rq before reading further).
Fields are present when known; a stream that did not originate in a
store has no chunk statistics.
.PP
An
.B entry
carries
.B src
(the path, which is also the prefix text output puts on each line) and
.B id
(the store's identity), under the same condition: both appear only where
an entry needs attributing \(em a read of one store attributes nothing,
because there is nothing to tell apart.
.PP
Both, rather than the path alone, because this is a PIPELINE format: a
stage that drops or rewrites
.B source
records would otherwise leave entries keyed by a path, which is neither
stable nor unique outside the response that produced it. An entry record
should say where it came from without depending on another record
surviving.
.TP
.B entry
Announces the next record: its verbatim payload. Fields:
.B len
(payload length in bytes, authoritative),
.B ts
(the entry's own timestamp, Unix milliseconds; absent when the entry
carries no parseable stamp),
.B wf
/
.B wl
(first/last write time of the chunk the entry COMPLETED in \(em an entry
becomes one when its stamped line closes, so a line split across two
chunks reports the second; present when known \(em a stream converted from
raw text has none),
.B chunk
(the number of that same chunk, in the SOURCE store \(em what a consumer's
cursor holds, and exact where a timestamp is not, chunk numbers only moving
forward. ABSENT means the producer read the entry from the live edge, where
no chunk exists yet: it may be delivered, but no resumable position inside
it can be recorded. It is a POSITION, not a fact about the entry, so unlike
.BR wf / wl
it is not carried into a destination store \(em an ingesting writer numbers
its own chunks, or two fan-in sources would interleave into a sequence that
is neither dense nor monotone),
.B src
(source path; present only in multi-source streams \(em single-source
streams name their source once, in
.BR stream-start / source ).
.TP
.B position
One per store EXAMINED, after the entries and before
.BR stream-end .
Fields:
.B path
and
.B id
(which store),
.B offset
(resume just here \(em ABSENT when this store delivered nothing, which is
not the same as offset zero), and
.BR chunks_read / chunks_selected
as progress.
.RS
.PP
The offset is absolute on the store's TAPE \(em what has ever left the
store, plus where the next undelivered entry sits in what remains \(em so
retention cannot move it, and unlike a timestamp it cannot COLLIDE. On a
fleet whose entries share a second, resuming by clock loses every entry
that shared the last one.
.PP
Hand these back as the query document's
.B cursor
and the next read continues exactly here; see
.BR timberfs-query-document (5).
Present for a store that matched NOTHING too \(em dropping those makes
the next page rescan them from the start of the window, which on a fleet
is most of the cost.
.PP
The round trip is LOSSLESS, and the case that decides it is a store which
delivers nothing on some page. Its offset is then the one it was RESUMED
from, not absent: nothing moved, so the position did not either. Absent
means there is no position at all \(em nothing delivered and nothing
resumed from \(em a store this search has consumed none of, which reads
from the start of the window.
.PP
⚠ The difference is not cosmetic. An offsetless entry in a
.B cursor
IS the start of the window, so a producer that omitted the offset for a
quiet store would have every consumer re-read it whole on the next page
and deliver every entry in it twice \(em while following this format's
own instruction to hand the positions back.
.RE
.TP
.B stream-end
Last record of every complete stream. Fields:
.B entries
(payloads emitted),
.B dropped
(entries verified outside the window),
.B chunks_read
/
.B chunks_total
(chunks actually READ, against the store's total \(em the two differ
exactly when a bound stopped the read, which is when a consumer is
counting; present when known),
.B status
\(em
.B exhausted
when everything in the selection was read, or
.B limited
when a bound the CALLER set stopped it short, in which case
.B limit
names WHICH one \(em
.BR max.entries ,
.B max.chunks
or
.BR deadline .
It names the bound that actually fired; it once said
.B max.entries
whatever had. A
.B deadline
is the one bound whose effect is not reproducible: the same request run
twice can stop in different places, so a consumer must read the per-store
.B position
records rather than assume the shape of the answer.
The two are not distinguishable from
.B entries
alone: a count reads as the whole answer either way, and a consumer that
cannot tell will present a truncated result as a complete one.
⚠ A bounded read never emits a PARTIAL entry. A chunk bound stops
mid-entry by construction \(em an entry may span a boundary \(em and the
entry being assembled when that happens is DROPPED rather than flushed:
half a stack trace presented as whole is not missing data but data that
never existed. A resume position names that entry's first byte, so the
next read delivers it complete. At genuine end-of-data the pending entry
is kept, because there it is merely unterminated: a log's last line need
not end in a newline.
.PP
.B limited
is reported only when more actually remained \(em an entry the sink
dropped because the cap was already reached, or chunks left unread because
the read stopped on it \(em so a selection that merely happened to equal
the cap still reports
.BR exhausted .
Its PRESENCE is the completeness marker:
a consumer that reaches end-of-input without seeing
.B stream-end
must treat the stream as truncated (the producer died, or the pipe
broke) and must not present the result as complete.
.SH ORDERING
Entries of ONE store always arrive in that store's own order. Between
stores, what is claimed depends on the read, and
.B stream-start
names it in
.BR order .
.TP
.B order=sequential
A bounded read: each store is drained before the next begins. There is
no order between stores, and none is implied \(em a store's entries are
contiguous, and the store boundaries fall where they fall.
.TP
.B order=arrival
A followed read
.RB ( \-\-follow ):
each store is polled in turn and whatever it has is emitted, so entries
appear in the order they reached the reader.
.PP
Neither is a timeline. A timeline needs entries sorted by the stamp they
CARRY, and no store is stored in that order \(em a buffered producer, a
late arrival or an imported file puts an entry on disk long after the
moment it describes. Sorting a stream by a key it is not already sorted
on means holding all of it first, so a producer that streams cannot also
sort: order by
.B ts
in the consumer, over a page it has whole.
.PP
Which is why a bounded read does not interleave. It could only key on
arrival, and over stores spanning years the two clocks have had that long
to diverge \(em a precise-looking sequence that is not the timeline it
resembles. A followed read has the opposite luck: everything is arriving
now, so
.B order=arrival
lands within a poll interval of the timeline without claiming to.
.PP
The ordering a bounded read gives holds for THAT answer only. A later
page resolves the store predicate again, and a store that has appeared
since carries entries older than the ones already delivered.
.SH VERSIONING
The stream is versioned by the
.B v
field of
.BR stream-start .
Consumers MUST ignore unknown keys and unknown record kinds \(em new
fields and kinds may appear within
.B v=1
and are always additive. A consumer seeing a
.B v
it does not know should refuse rather than guess.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR timberfs (1),
.BR timber\-filter (1),
.BR timber\-otlp (1),
.BR timberfs\-query\-document (5)
